From: thunder
Subject: [PATCH] u_ether: linearize the skb on the single-packet TX path

eth_start_xmit() sends skb->data with skb->len bytes when packet
aggregation (multi_pkt_xfer) is off, which only works for a linear skb.
MediaTek's u_ether sets NETIF_F_SG and NETIF_F_GSO, and this 4.19 tree no
longer drops SG for devices without checksum offload, so TCP builds skbs
whose payload sits in page fragments: the first ~2 KB of each write is in
the linear head, the rest is not. The gadget then sends the bytes that
follow the head in memory instead of the payload.

On the host, the TCP checksum fails, conntrack marks the segment INVALID
and a stateful firewall drops it. Symptom: over the USB link, ping and the
first segments of a TCP connection pass, then the stream stalls (the SSH
key exchange never completes, a 20 KB send delivers exactly the first
1024 bytes). Wi-Fi was unaffected because its driver handles fragments.

Found on 2026-10-10 with the host logging "[UFW BLOCK] ... SRC=172.16.42.1
... PROTO=TCP SPT=22" for replies of a connection the host had opened,
while 300 back-to-back 1400-byte pings came back intact.

---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/u_ether.c	2026-10-10 17:42:02.565778123 -0300
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/u_ether.c	2026-10-10 17:42:02.578813489 -0300
@@ -742,6 +742,18 @@
 		return NETDEV_TX_OK;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The single-packet path below hands skb->data with skb->len bytes to
+	 * the controller, which is only right for a linear skb. This driver sets
+	 * NETIF_F_SG, so TCP queues its payload in page fragments, and whatever
+	 * follows the linear head in memory goes out on the wire instead.
+	 */
+	if (!multi_pkt_xfer && skb_linearize(skb)) {
+		dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
+		dev->net->stats.tx_dropped++;
+		return NETDEV_TX_OK;
+	}
+
 	/* apply outgoing CDC or RNDIS filters */
 	if (skb && !is_promisc(cdc_filter)) {
 		u8		*dest = skb->data;
